description:
Maintainer (2026-10-13): when iterating, skip the long example benchmarks whose kernels did not change.

Key per example (all must match to reuse a stored result):
- every kernel module's .beam MD5 (module_info(:md5)): covers the C source (compiled in as __defk_c_source__) and the generated Elixir wrappers (argument decoding, NIF call) that are part of the timing;
- the library cache key inputs (Defk.Build: cflags, defk_runtime.h, cc + version, ERTS version, sanitizer mode);
- the example script's hash (the Elixir baseline and inputs);
- the script's Mix.install dependency versions (often the competing library);
- Defk.Bench's own MD5, OTP and Elixir versions, and the machine (CPU model, core count).

Rules:
- Store only quiet-machine results; a provisional (loaded) run is never reused, so it re-measures next time.
- --bench reuses cached results by default and says which ones it reused; --bench --fresh measures everything. A --snapshot table marks reused rows (with their date).
- Cache outside git (e.g. _build/<env>/defk_bench/), one file per example.
- Do NOT cache agreement/correctness checks (random draws add coverage each run; the build cache already skips the C compile) or compiler tests.

Test: change only an example's Elixir baseline -> re-measured; change nothing -> reused; touch defk_runtime.h -> all re-measured; a loaded run -> not stored.
